  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">Surges in military aid concentrate procurement and integration of AI-capable hardware and software with a limited set of defense contractors and cloud/hosting providers, increasing concentration of economic and operational exposure to those actors.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_agent_systemic_risks</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="iran_hormuz_flashpoint::maritime_attack_frequenc" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A surge in military aid to Ukraine forces the U.S. and allied navies to reassign ships and escorts to the European theatre, reducing persistent deterrent presence in the Persian Gulf and thereby enabling more maritime attacks on commercial shipping.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_flashpoint</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="us_china_strategic_rival::strategic_tension_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in Western military aid to Ukraine causes Beijing to perceive a more assertive US-led security posture and to respond by hardening its own military-diplomatic stance, increasing overall strategic tension between Washington and Beijing.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flow" target="us_china_strategic_rival::tech_flow_regulation" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Larger and more sophisticated transfers of materiel into the conflict prompt US policymakers to expand and tighten export controls and investment screening on dual‑use technologies to prevent re‑routing or proliferation, increasing regulation affecting China.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
