<edge source="iran_hormuz_flashpoint::physical_supply_disrupti" target="global_bond_market_shock::sovereign_bond_yields" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Acute physical disruption to oil infrastructure reduces export revenue and triggers immediate oil-price spikes and higher headline inflation expectations plus larger fiscal support needs, which together raise sovereign inflation and sovereign-risk premia and push up government bond yields within markets' re-pricing horizon.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_hormuz_flashpoint</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_hormuz_flashpoint::physical_supply_disrupti" target="us_china_strategic_rival::diplomatic_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Significant physical disruptions to regional oil infrastructure force both Washington and Beijing into urgent high‑level consultations and coordinated diplomatic initiatives (e.g., crisis talks, joint statements, requests for transit guarantees) to stabilize markets and shipping routes.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_hormuz_flashpoint</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
